                <pb facs="0428" n="330" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg330"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Jackson to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1900-07-26"><hi rendition="#italic">July 26,
                        1900</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1320.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that on the
                    receipt, on the 20th instant, of your instruction No. 1067, of July 6, 1900, the
                    embassy communicated to the Imperial foreign office the text of the telegram
                    addresed by the President to His Majesty the German Emperor relative to the
                    murder of Baron von Ketteler, the German minister at Pekin, quoting in its note
                    the last paragraph of the instruction above mentioned. To-day a note has been
                    received in reply, in which the foreign office expresses its most sincere thanks
                    for the information and adds that the President’s telegram had been answered by
                    the Emperor himself.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John B. Jackson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
